\section{Introduction}

Employability is one of the most important outcomes of higher education, and for many students it becomes a concern from the very start of their university journey, not only after graduation. Across the world, factors such as the quality of education, university ranking, and the skills gained during study all play a significant role in shaping a graduate's employability(Fakulne, 2021). As competition increases and labour markets become harder to enter, students everywhere are looking for a degree that does more than provide knowledge, they want an experience that helps them stand out from other graduates. 

In some countries, such as China, admissions to a strong master's program is highly competitive, and graduates then enter an equally competitive domestic job market. In others, such as India, the issue is more about the shortage of strong master's-level programs and institutions where students can genuinely specialize in their field paired with weaker salary prospects for graduates in the domestic job market. 

The UK is one of the main destinations students choose. In 2024/25, 685,565 international students were enrolled at UK higher education institutions, making up around 24\% of the total student population (HESA, 2024/25). The UK has strong higher education system, well-established career support services, and high number of globally ranked universities: 17 UK universities were placed in the world's top 100 in the Q World University Ranking 2026 (QS,2026). A 2021 survey by the Higher Education Policy Institute (HEPI) and Kaplan International Pathways, covering over 1,000 international students across 118 UK universities, found that 92\% students rated the employability skills they expected to gain were important or very important when choosing where to study, and 82\% said the same about the careers support they expected (HEPI, Kaplan International Pathways, 2021). This suggests that students are not only choosing the UK for the qualification itself, but for the combination of degree and experience that they believe will make them more competitive both internationally and back in their home countries. 

Fakulne (2021) found that employability is consistently the main reason international students give for studying abroad, and many students in her study also planned or hoped to stay in the UK to work, at least for a period, after finishing their studies A U degree and UK work experience are therefore seen by many students as a way to strengthen heir position both int he UK job market and int heir home country's once they return. 

However, arriving in the UK is only the beginning of this journey. Students still have to compete for jobs, as it is not automatically guaranteed that studying in the UK will lead to strong employment outcomes (Di Pietro, 2019, cited in Fakulne, 2021).

This raises an important question: what actually determines whether an international student is able to find employment after their studies, and what increases their chances of doing so? This dissertation uses data on international students in the UK to examine which factors are associated with employment status after graduation. The findings are intended to help universities better understand the gap between the student experience and the demands of the labour market, so that they can offer more targeted support and guidance. This would allow universities to differentiate themselves not simply as providers of education, but as institutions that genuinely support students' employability and post-graduate career outcomes. It will also help students understand which factors matter the most for their own transition to employment, that they know where to focus their efforts during their studies. 

