\section{Description of the methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Classification methods}

 
Classification methods are prediction models and algorithms used to classify or categorize objects based on their measurements. Analysis typically begins with "labeled" data, which are observations with measurements on one or more explanatory variables (features) as well as a label variable (outcome) indicating which class the object belongs to. The goal is to construct a classification rule from these labeled observations that can predict the class of future, unlabeled observations that share the same explanatory variables.

In this dissertation, this goal translates into the building of a classification rule for \textbf{Employment\_Status (Employed vs. unemployed)} using the features of each graduate, such as GPA, Internship\_Experience, and Education\_Level.


\subsubsection{K-Nearest Neighbor (KNN)}

K-Nearest Neighbours is a nonparametric classification method. For each point, we look at its k nearest labelled points and predict its class to be that of the majority of its neighbours, we use the observations in the training set closest to a given point. This requires four steps: deciding the value of k; defining closeness via a distance metric; sorting distances to determine the nearest neighbours; and assigning the class held by the majority of those neighbours.

Several distance metrics exist, including Euclidean, Manhattan, and Mahalanobis distance. This analysis uses the standard choice, Euclidean distance, defined as the square root of the sum of squared differences between two points across all predictors. For $p$-dimensional continuous variables, the distance between two points $x$ and $x_i$ is defined as:

\begin{equation}
d(x, x_i) = \sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{p} (x_j - x_{ij})^2}
\end{equation}

Having defined distance, the value of k must then be chosen, and this choice creates a bias-variance trade-off. When k is small, each prediction depends on only a handful of nearby points, $k=1$ relies on a single neighbor. This makes the classifier very flexible, a single mislabeled point can flip a prediction. This instability is high variance, and the classifier is said to overfit. When k is large, predictions are averaged over many neighbors instead. This makes predictions stable, so variance is low. The classifier becomes too simple to capture the true classification boundary. This is high bias, and the classifier is said to underfit. The aim is therefore to choose a value of k that balances these two effects.

To select k objectively rather than arbitrarily, a 10 fold cross-validation was used: for each candidate value of $k=1,\dots,50$ the training data was split into 10 folds, the model was fitted on 9 folds and evaluated on the remaining fold, and this was repeated across all 10 folds to obtain an average cross-validated accuracy for that k. The value of k that maximized cross-validated accuracy was then selected for the final model.


\subsubsection{Classification Tree}

Classification trees are another type of non-parametric classification method, also known as partitioning methods, since the result is a partition of the feature space (the set of possible values of the explanatory variables). Classification trees involve two steps. First, the feature space is divided into a number of disjoint, non-overlapping regions. Second, for a given test observation, its class is predicted to be the most common class among the training observations in the region to which it belongs.

A tree is built from a sequence of features, each with a cut-off point chosen on that feature; a given feature can appear more than once in the tree, with different cut-off points at different splits. Each split leads either to a further split or to a terminal (leaf) node, where a class is predicted. Once the tree is built, each leaf is assigned the class held by the majority of training observations that fall into it. To make a prediction, an observation starts at the root node and follows the branches determined by whether it satisfies each split condition, until it reaches a leaf.

Choosing the best feature and cut-off point at each split requires a measure of node impurity to optimize. This analysis uses the \textbf{Gini index}, defined as:




\begin{equation}
Gini = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \hat{p}_k(1 - \hat{p}_k)
\end{equation}

where $K$ is the total number of classes and $\hat{p}_k$ is the proportion of training observations in the node belonging to class $k$. A node containing only one class has a Gini index of $0$ (pure), while a node with an even mix of classes has a higher Gini index (impure); at each stage, the split chosen is the one producing the greatest reduction in impurity. This approach is greedy where at each step, the best split is chosen given the splits already made so far, without looking ahead to whether a different choice would lead to a better tree in some future step.

A fully grown tree tends to overfit the training data, so it is \textbf{pruned} to reduce complexity. Pruning is controlled by a complexity parameter ($cp$), and two strategies were compared to select its value: the \textbf{minimum error rule}, which prunes the tree back to the point where the cross-validated error is at a minimum; and the \textbf{1-SE (smallest tree) rule}, which first adds one standard error to the minimum cross-validated error to obtain a threshold, then selects the largest value of ($cp$) (the smallest tree) whose cross-validated error falls within that threshold. The 1-SE rule prunes slightly further than the minimum-error point, typically yielding a smaller, more interpretable tree.



\paragraph{Bagging and Random Forest}

Bagging, or bootstrap aggregation, is an ensemble technique that reduces the variance of a prediction by combining the results of many models fitted to different bootstrap samples of the training data (samples drawn with replacement from the original data). For classification, trees are grown, each on a different bootstrap sample, and the final prediction is made by majority vote across all trees. Bagging works particularly well for high-variance, low-bias procedures such as classification trees, since averaging over many trees smooths out the instability of any single tree.

Random forest is a modification of bagging that further de-correlates the trees in the ensemble: rather than each tree splitting on the best feature from the full set of predictors at every split, random forest restricts each split to a random subset of the available features. This prevents strong predictors from dominating every tree in the ensemble, producing a more diverse set of trees whose averaged predictions further reduce variance compared to standard bagging.

\subsubsection{Support Vector Machine}


Support Vector Machines (SVM) are a supervised classification technique that finds a boundary separating classes by maximising the margin between them. The boundary is a hyperplane, and the margin is the perpendicular distance from the hyperplane to the nearest point(s) of either class. Where multiple hyperplanes could separate the classes, SVM selects the one that maximises this distance,the largest margin, \textbf{maximal margin hyperplane}.

Once the optimal hyperplane has been found, with estimated weight vector $\hat{w}$ and estimated bias term $\hat{b}$, classification of a new observation $x_{new}$ reduces to determining which side of the hyperplane it falls on. This is given by:

\begin{equation}
\hat{g}(x_{new}) = \text{sign}(\hat{w}^T x_{new} + \hat{b})
\end{equation}


where $\text{sign}(\cdot)$ returns $+1$ for positive values and $-1$ for negative values. That is, $x_{new}$ is substituted into the estimated hyperplane equation, and the sign of the resulting value determines the predicted class: observations on one side of the hyperplane are assigned class $-1$, and observations on the other side are assigned class $+1$.



The boundary described so far is linear. SVM can be extended to nonlinear boundaries by mapping the input features into a higher-dimensional space using a \textbf{kernel function}, within which a linear boundary is fitted; when mapped back to the original feature space, this linear boundary corresponds to a nonlinear boundary. Common kernel choices include the \textbf{linear kernel} (no transformation, producing a straight-line boundary), the \textbf{polynomial kernel}, and the \textbf{radial (Gaussian) kernel}, each producing increasingly flexible, curved decision boundaries. The choice of kernel is typically guided by whether the classes appear linearly separable in the original feature space and by comparing predictive performance across kernels.


\subsection{Model Selection}

For our third and fourth research questions, model selection techniques were used to ensure that the analysis retained only the variables with the most interpretable and significant effects on Employment status and Salary. There are several different model selection techniques; for this analysis we focus on \textbf{stepwise selection} and \textbf{shrinkage methods}.


\subsubsection{Stepwise Selection}

Stepwise selection has 3 types: forward, backward and both-directions. For this analysis, the Both-direction was used. In Both-Direction stepwise selection was used. We start with a full model containing all predictors). At each step, the algorithm considers both removing  variables currently in the model that contributes the least( based on AIC/BIC criteria) and adding variables not currently in the model. It keeps iterating by adding or removing one variable at a time until no further step improves the criterion, effectively reaching the lowest achievable AIC/BIC value.

\subsubsection{Shrinkage Methods}


Unlike stepwise selection, which searches over a discrete subsets of predictions, shrinkage methods fit the full model but penalize the coefficients/. This constraint reduces variance at the cost of a small increase in bias and depending on the penalty used we can also perform variable selection by shrinking some coefficient exactly to zero.

A general way to express this is to minimize the residual sum of squares subject to a combination of two penalty terms: 

\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta} = \arg\min_{\beta} \left\{ \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2 + \lambda_1 \sum_{j=1}^{p}|\beta_j| + \lambda_2 \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2 \right\}
\label{eq:general-penalty}
\end{equation}

The two penalty terms behave differently:

\begin{itemize}
\item The term $\sum_{j=1}^{p}|\beta_j|$ is the \textbf{L1 penalty}: the sum of the \textit{absolute values} of the coefficients.
\item The term $\sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2$ is the \textbf{L2 penalty}: the sum of the \textit{squared} coefficients.
\end{itemize}

The tuning parameters $\lambda_1$ and $\lambda_2$ control how much weight is given to each penalty.

\paragraph{Lasso}


Lasso is is a penalized regression method. Instead of testing subsets of variables like stepwise selection, it fits all the predictors at once but adds a penalty to the loss function that constrains the total size of the coefficients( the sum of their absolute values), this penalty shrinkage the coefficients to zero, so it automatically performs variable selection technique. it takes care of the bias-variance trade off by continuous shrinkage

Setting $\lambda_2 = 0$ in Equation~\ref{eq:general-penalty} leaves only the L1 penalty, giving the lasso:

\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}^{lasso} = \arg\min_{\beta} \left\{ \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2 + \lambda_1 \sum_{j=1}^{p}|\beta_j| \right\}
\end{equation}



\paragraph{Ridge}

Ridge regression estimates coefficients by minimizing the residual sum of squares subject to a penalty of the sum of squared coefficients. Unlike Lasso, it does not shrink coefficients exactly to zero, it only makes them as small as possible and therefore does not perform variable selection.

Setting $\lambda_1 = 0$ in Equation~\ref{eq:general-penalty} leaves only the L2 penalty.  As $\lambda_2$ increases, coefficients are shrunk further toward zero, though never exactly to zero:

\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}^{ridge} = \arg\min_{\beta} \left\{ \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2 + \lambda_2 \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2 \right\}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Elastic Net}

The elastic net combines both the L1 and L2 penalties, allowing it to perform coefficient shrinkage and variable selection simultaneously as in Equation~\ref{eq:general-penalty}.

\subsection{Statistical Models}

\subsubsection{Multiple Linear Regression}

Multiple linear regression models a continuous response variable as a linear combination of predictors. For salary, the model takes the form:

\begin{equation}
y_i = \beta_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij} + \epsilon_i
\end{equation}

where $y_i$ is the salary of the $i$th individual, $x_{ij}$ are the predictor values, $\beta_j$ are the coefficients to be estimated, and $\epsilon_i$ is the error term, assumed to be independently and normally distributed with mean zero and constant variance.

\subsubsection{Logistic Regression}

For employment status, a binary outcome (employed/unemployed), ordinary least squares is not appropriate, since predictions are not constrained to lie between 0 and 1. Instead, logistic regression models the probability of the outcome using the logit link function:

\begin{equation}
\log\left(\frac{p_i}{1-p_i}\right) = \beta_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij}
\end{equation}

where $p_i = P(y_i = 1 \mid x_i)$ is the probability that individual $i$ is employed. 

Coefficients are estimated by maximum likelihood rather than least squares. Exponentiating a coefficient, $\exp(\beta_j)$, gives the odds ratio associated with a one-unit increase in $x_j$: values greater than 1 indicate the predictor increases the odds of employment, while values less than 1 indicate it decreases the odds.

\subsubsection{Generalized Additive Models (GAM)}
Multiple linear regression assumes a strictly linear relationship between each predictor and the response. Generalized additive models (GAMs) relax this assumption by allowing continuous predictors to enter the model through smooth, non-linear functions while keeping the model additive and interpretable:

\begin{equation}
y_i = \beta_0 + \sum_{k} f_k(x_{ik}) + \sum_{j} \beta_j x_{ij} + \epsilon_i
\end{equation}

where $f_k(\cdot)$ are smooth functions (typically splines) fitted to continuous predictors such as GPA, age, and years since graduation, and $\beta_j$ are ordinary linear coefficients for categorical predictors. The smoothness of each $f_k$ is controlled by a penalty term, tuned automatically via generalized cross-validation (GCV) or restricted maximum likelihood (REML), preventing overfitting while allowing the model to capture non-linear patterns that a purely linear model would miss.

\subsection{Model Evaluation Metrics}

After applying a classification method on a data set, for each observation we predict a class, so this prediction may be right where we are getting the predicted class same as the true class or the prediction is wrong were we get the predicted class different than the true class. For this we need to evaluate the performance of the classification method: how good it is at classifying observation, mans how good the method is overall. 

\subsubsection{Confusion Matrix}

It is a cross-tabulation of predicted class versus true class, where the$ij$-th element in the table gives the number of observations that were actually in class $i$ but predicted to be in class $j$. 

We'll consider four different cases for this matrix. Assume we have two classes: sick ($y=1$) as the True class, and healthy($y=0$) as the False class. We want to predict whether a person is sick or healthy. A positive prediction means the person is predicted to be sick , and a negative prediction means the person is predicted to be healthy.

These four cases fill the confusion matrix as follows Table~\ref{tbl:confusionMatrix}:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{True Positive (TP):} a person is originally sick and predicted to be sick --- correctly identified.
\item \textbf{False Positive (FP):} a person is healthy but predicted to be sick --- incorrectly identified.
\item \textbf{True Negative (TN):} a person is healthy and predicted to be healthy --- correctly rejected.
\item \textbf{False Negative (FN):} a person is sick but predicted to be healthy --- incorrectly rejected.
\end{itemize}

