From these values, we can find performance metrics:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Sensitivity/Recall (True Positive Rate)}: the proportion of all observations that we correctly predicted as \textbf{positive}, $TP/(TP+FN)$.
\item \textbf{Specificity (True Negative Rate)}: the proportion of all observations that were correctly predicted as \textbf{negative}, $TN/(TN+FP)$.
\item \textbf{Accuracy (Correct Classification Rate)}: the number of correctly classified observations in their true class, whether positive or negative; this lies on the diagonal of the table, so we take the trace of the diagonal over the total number of observations, $(TP+TN)/n$. 
\textit{(Misclassification Rate is $1-\text{accuracy}$, which is the trace of the values that lie on the off-diagonal over the total number of observations.)}
\item \textbf{Precision (Positive Predictive Value)}: the proportion of correctly predicted positive observations, $TP/(TP+FP)$.
\item \textbf{$F_1$ Score}: a single-number summary of precision and recall.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{ROC and Precision-Recall Curves}

\begin{itemize}

\item \textbf{ROC (Receiver Operating Characteristic):} commonly used to assess the trade-off between sensitivity and specificity for a binary classifier. It plots Sensitivity (True Positive Rate) on the y-axis against 1 − Specificity (False Positive Rate) on the x-axis across all classification thresholds, capturing both how many actual positives are correctly caught and how many actual negatives are incorrectly classified as positive.Overall performance is summarized by the area under the curve (AUC), with values closer to 1 indicating better discrimination between classes.

\item \textbf{Precision-Recall Curve:} used when the class distribution is imbalanced, as is the case in this dataset (see @fig-employment in @sec-EDA)(see Figure~\ref{fig:employment} in Section~\ref{sec:EDA}). It plots Recall on the x-axis against Precision on the y-axis across all classification thresholds, capturing how many of the actual positives were correctly caught, and capturing how many of the predicted positives were actually correct. This focuses specifically on performance for the \textbf{positive class only}.Overall performance is summarized by the area under the curve (AUC), with values closer to 1 indicating stronger precision-recall trade-off.

\end{itemize}

