\section{Analysis of the data}

\subsection{Research Question 1: Classification models to predict employment status}
\label{sec:RS1}

For this question, as explained previously, Employment Status was filtered to the Employed/ Unemployed categories to frame this as a binary classification problem. Non-informative columns were removed: Region\_of\_Study (constant at "UK" after filtering), and Salary and Job\_Sector, since these are outcomes of employment status rather than predictors of it.

\subsubsection{K-Nearest Neighbours Method}

Employment\_Status was set as the response variable (Y), with the remaining variables forming the predictor set (X). Categorical predictors in X were 
converted into dummy variables, while the continuous predictors (GPA, Age, Years\_Since\_Graduation) were identified separately so that they could later be scaled. A seed was fixed at 1 throughout to ensure 
reproducible results.


The dataset was then split into 80\% training and 20\% test sets. Since KNN 
relies on distance calculations between observations, unscaled variables with larger ranges would disproportionately dominate the distance metric, so the continuous predictors were standardized. To avoid data leakage, the scaling parameters (mean and standard deviation) were computed using only the training data, and these same parameters were then applied to scale both X continuous values of the training and test sets.


\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.74\linewidth]{Rplot12.png}
    \caption{10-Fold Cross-Validation Accuracy vs k}
    \label{fig:knn-cv}
\end{figure}

A K-nearest neighbors algorithm was applied, testing fifty candidate values of $k$ (1 to 50) using 10-fold cross-validation, as described in Section~\ref{sec:methods}. For each candidate value of $k$, the training data was split into 10 folds: one fold was held out as validation while the remaining nine were used for training, and this was repeated so that each fold served as the validation set exactly once. This produced 10 accuracy estimates per value of $k$, which were averaged to give a cross-validated accuracy for that $k$. Repeating this process across all fifty candidate values of $k$ produced the cross-validated accuracy curve shown in Figure~\ref{fig:knn-cv}. The value of $k$ that maximized cross-validated accuracy, $k=28$, was selected as the final model, meaning each prediction is based on the $28$ nearest neighboring observations.

Using this value of $k$, class predictions were made on the test 
set, and test accuracy was computed as the proportion of correctly 
classified observations. 

\subsubsection{Classification Trees}


As with the KNN model, the data was split into 80\% training and 20\% test sets, using the same seed for consistency across methods. Employment\_Status was set as a factor to indicate a classification, rather than regression task.

A full classification tree was first grown with cost complexity set to zero $cp=0$, letting it split repeatedly until every terminal node was pure. This produced an extremely large, complex tree, a clear sign of overfitting.The tree was therefore pruned, using the approaches described
in Section~\ref{sec:methods}: the minimum cross-validated error rule and the 1-SE rule.

Pruning via the minimum-error rule selected $cp=0.0004$, producing a tree with $21$ splits. The 1-SE rule, which first adds one standard error to the minimum cross-validated error to obtain a threshold and then selects the smallest tree whose error falls within that threshold, selected a smaller, less complex tree. A 2-SE variant was also considered, using two standard errors instead of one to push toward an even smaller tree; this produced only a marginal further reduction, differing from the 1-SE tree by a small number of nodes rather than a meaningfully different structure. Given this negligible difference, the 1-SE tree was retained rather than the 2-SE version, as the standard and more commonly used choice.

To decide between the pruning strategies, and to check whether a single tree was competitive with ensemble methods, four models were compared on test accuracy: the minimum-error tree, the 1-SE tree, bagging, and random forest, both grown as 500-tree ensembles on bootstrap samples of the training data, as described in Section~\ref{sec:methods}. Table~\ref{tbl:tree-comparison} shows the minimum-error tree achieved the highest accuracy at 0.9073, with bagging and random forest at  0.8966 and 0.8981 respectively, and the 1-SE tree close behind at 0.9069.


Despite not achieving the highest accuracy, the 1-SE tree was selected as the model to report in detail as it offers a substantially simpler, more interpretable partition of the feature space. 


\subsubsection{Support Vector Machine (SVM)}

As a further approach to classifying employment status, we fitted support vector machine (SVM) models with four different kernels: linear, radial(gaussian), polynomial of degree 2, and polynomial of degree 3. A seed of 1 was set again for each of these models for reproducibility. This was by far the most computationally expensive step in the analysis, in particular, tuning the linear kernel took over two hours to run. Given this cost,we focused on trying different kernels rather than tuning.

Each of the four fitted models was then used to predict employment status on the same test set used for the classification tree, and we compared their test set accuracy as shown in Table~\ref{tbl:SVM}.

Table~\ref{tbl:SVM} shows that the radial(gaussian) kernel achieved the highest test set accuracy among the four models. A radial kernel can capture more flexible, non-linear decision boundaries than a linear kernel, without imposing the specific polynomial structure of the degree 2 and degree 3 kernels, so it is reasonable choice when the true decision boundary is unknown.

