\subsection{Research Question 3: Which factors are the most important predictors of employment outcomes?}
\label{sec:RS3}


In this section, we investigate which factors are the most important predictors of employment status. To answer this, we identify the best-fitting model using two approaches explained earlier in Section~\ref{sec:methods}: stepwise subset selection and Lasso regression. 

Since employment status is a binary response, we frame this as logistic regression problem, where the goal is to estimate the probability of being employed versus unemployed. We first constructed a binary response variable, coding "Employed" as 1 and " Unemployed" as 0, and removed the original `Employmnet\_Status` variable now that the `Employmnet\_binary` served as the response variable. A seed set to 1 to ensure reproducibility of results. Since we are also interested in the predictive accuracy of the resulting model, we also split the data into training and test data sets. 


We first fit a full logistic model containing all predictors and applied \texttt{stepAIC()}, a both-direction stepwise selection using AIC. This flagged `Gender` as a non-significant predictor, with p-values above 0.05 for both the Male and Other categories. To confirm this, we compared the AIC-selected model against a nested model with Gender removed using a likelihood-ratio test (\texttt{anova()}). After selecting the nested model without `Gender`, we generated the predictions on the test data and calculated the classification accuracy. As a second stepwise approach, we repeated this procedure using BIC criteria instead of AIC, again generating predictions and accuracy on the test data.  

The resulting accuracies were 0.8533724 for the AIC model after removing Gender and 0.8533724 for the BIC model, which are exactly the same.

The second approach considered was Lasso regression which, as discussed earlier in Section~\ref{sec:methods}, automatically performs variable selection by shrinking the coefficients of uninformative predictors to exactly zero. To fit the LASSO model, the data set was split into training and test data set of the predictor matrix `X` and response vector `Y` (Employment\_binary). Since our primary interest was LASSO, we also fitted ridge regression ($\alpha=0$) and elastic net ($\alpha=0.5$) for comparison purposes; the same procedure applied to LASSo was applied to these two models, but only LASSO results are discussed in details in this report. The plot below , Figure~\ref{fig:lasso}, shows the coefficient path of LASSO model fitted with \texttt{glmnet()}, using a binary family and $\alpha=1$.
