In this plot, coefficient values are shown on the y-axis, and $-\log(\lambda)$ is shown on the bottom x-axis. Moving from left to right, $-\log(\lambda)$ increases, meaning $\lambda$ decreases so the penalty on the coefficients is decreasing(the far right of the plot corresponds to almost no penalty). The top axis shows the number of non-zero coefficients in the model at each point along the path: at the far left(strong penalty) with only 2 predictors remain non-zero; at far right( low penalty), 25 predictors remain non-zero. Each colored line represents one predictor, or, for a categorical variable, one dummy-coded level. 


The direction of each line indicates the sign of that predictor's coefficient on the log-odds of being employed. The numbers on the right edge of the plot correspond to column indices of `X\_train`. For example, tracing index 28 shows that this is GPA. This line is moving upward- positive coefficient- means the predictor is positively associated with employment: for instance, a higher GPA is associated with higher portability of being employed.

This plot illustrated how the number of non-zero coefficients change across values of $\lambda$, but it does not identify the best-performing model. For that, we use cross-validation via \texttt{cv.glmnet()} to find the best $\lambda$. \\

After applying `cv.glmnet()`, we selected the cross-validated lambda value that minimizes deviance, `lambda.min` equal to 0.0008, and refit the model using `glmnet()` with this $\lambda$ value. We then evaluated the model's performance in predicting employment status on the test set. The same cross-validation procedure was applied to select $\lambda$ for the ridge and elastic net models. We then compared the accuracy of the three shrinkage methods to determine which performed best.
From Table~\ref{tbl:result-shrinkagemethods}, LASSO achieved the highest accuracy at 0.8519.


Using LASSO as variable-selection filter, we examined the coefficients of the LASSO model to identify which predictors were shrunk to exactly zero. The following variables were shrunk to zero: `Age`, all three categories of `Visa\_Type`, only two of the eight `Country\_of\_Origin` categories, and one of the six `Field\_of\_Study` categories. We conclude it was reasonable to drop `Age` and `Visa\_Type`.

We then refit a GLM using all predictors except `Age` and `Visa\_Type`. Examining this model's summary, `Gender` had a p-value above 0.05, indicating non-significance; we therefore removed `Gender` and used a likelihood-ratio test (\texttt{anova()}) to confirm that the nested model (without `Gender`) was preferred. We repeated this process over several steps, sequentially removing `Field\_of\_Study` and then `Country\_of\_Origin` (in that order), arriving at a final model containing `Education\_Level`, `Language\_Proficiency`, `University\_Ranking`, `GPA`, `Years\_Since\_Graduation`, and `Internship\_Experience`.

A notable finding was that this final LASSO-informed model, the BIC-selected model, and the AIC-selected model (after removing Gender) all converged on the same predictors, coefficients, p-values, and test-set accuracy of \textbf{0.8533724}. All three variable-selection approaches produced an identical final model.

We next explored whether meaningful interaction effects existed among predictors, motivated by sensible reasoning. A higher GPA may increase the likelihood of securing an internship, motivating an interaction between `GPA` and `Internship\_Experience`. The likelihood of securing an internship may also depend on university ranking, as students from higher-ranked universities may be perceived by employers as more capable or better prepared, and education level may moderate the effect of language proficiency on employment outcomes.

We compared this interaction model against the final LASSO-informed model to assess which better predicted employment status.

