\subsection{Research Question 4: Which factors are strongly associated with salary?}
\label{sec:RS4}

To this section, we investigate which factors are strongly associated with salary. To do this, we restricted the sample to employed graduates only by removing observations with Salary$=0$, since these correspond to graduates who were unemployed or still in education. The resulting dataset also excluded `Job\_Sector`, `Employment\_Status` which has only one category- Employed, and `Region\_of\_Study` from the predictor set.

As in  Section~\ref{sec:RS3}, we used split the data set to training and test sets and applied both-direction stepwise selection based on BIC to a full model containing all remaining predictors. Since salary is a strictly positive, right-skewed variable by looking at the box plot in Figure~\ref{fig:salary}, we also considered whether a log-transformation of `Salary` improved model fit: we fitted a second full model using $\log(\text{Salary})$ as the response, and again applied BIC stepwise selection. The residual diagnostics for both models: the untransformed model (BIC\_model) and the log-transformed model (BIC\_log\_salary) are compared in Figure~\ref{fig:residualplot}.


Comparing the two sets of diagnostics, the log-transformed model's residuals are noticeably right-skewed, and its Q–Q plot departs clearly from the line at both tails, indicating that the log-transformed residuals are not well approximated by a normal distribution. The untransformed model's residuals, by contrast, are reasonably symmetric around zero, and its Q–Q plot follows the line closely across almost its entire range. We therefore proceeded with the untransformed salary model.

One notable feature of the untransformed model's residuals-versus-fitted plot is that the points form clusters. To investigate this, we plotted the same residuals against fitted values, coloring points by Education\_Level as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:cluster}. It shows that this clustering is explained almost entirely by `Education\_Level`. PhD graduates form a distinct cluster at the highest fitted salaries, Master's graduates form a lower cluster, and Bachelor's and Diploma graduates cluster together at the lowest fitted salaries, consistent with the expectation that further study is associated with higher earnings.



