As a second approach, we fitted a generalized additive model (GAM) using the \texttt{mgcv} package with \texttt{method = "REML"}, restricted to the variables retained by the BIC-selected linear model.

After fitting the initial additive model, we examined the smooth function plots to check for non-linear relationships between the continuous predictors (`Age`, `Years\_since\_Graduation` and `GPA`) and salary. GPA was the only predictor showing a clearly non-linear effect on salary.


We then examined the model summary and removed non-significant predictors one at a time, confirming each removal with a likelihood-ratio test (\texttt{anova()}) between the reduced and full models. Following this procedure, we sequentially removed `Gender`, `Visa\_Type`, `Field\_of\_Study`, `Country\_of\_Origin`, and finally the linear continuous terms `Years\_Since\_Graduation` and `Age`, finishing at a final model containing `GPA`, `Internship\_Experience`, `Education\_Level`, `University\_Ranking`, and `Language\_Proficiency`.

For this step-by-step comparison, each candidate model was refitted using \texttt{method = "ML"} rather than \texttt{"REML"}, since the models being compared differ in which predictors they include, so REML likelihoods aren't valid for that comparison. ML likelihoods don't have this restriction. Once the final variable set was settled, the model was refit with \texttt{method = "REML"}.

The plot below shows the non linear effect with salary (Figure~\ref{fig:salaryGPA}). The estimated smooth term for GPA has an effective degrees of freedom (edf) of approximately 8.70. This is far from the edf$= 1$ that would indicate a linear relationship, confirming a genuinely non-linear, fairly complex relationship between GPA and salary. 

The direction of this relationship is unexpected: across most of the GPA range, a higher GPA is associated with a lower predicted salary. One plausible, non-statistical explanation is that GPA and career-relevant employability skills are not the same thing. Students who prioritize maximizing their GPA may spend relatively more time on coursework and exam preparation and relatively less time on internships, networking, or other extracurricular activities that employers may weight heavily, and that GPA does not capture at all. 

We also examined whether allowing for interaction effects changed the model. Using the same interaction terms considered in section ~\ref{sec:RS3} (GPA:Internship\_Experience, Internship\_Experience: University\_Ranking, and Education\_Level:Language\_Proficiency), a likelihood-ratio test indicated that the interaction model provided a significantly better fit than the additive model. 

Fitting this interaction model, however, changed the shape of the estimated GPA effect quite substantially, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:GPA}.


