\section{Conclusions and discussion}

This dissertation examined what shapes employment outcomes for international graduates int he UK using classification and regression models. 
Across the classification methods tested, the pruned classification tree (1-SE rule) was the best-performing model overall, with both the highest accuracy and the greatest interpretability among the models compared. The most significant predictors of employment status were \texttt{Education\_Level}, \texttt{Language\_Proficiency}, \texttt{University\_Ranking}, \texttt{GPA}, \texttt{Years\_Since\_Graduation}, and \texttt{Internship\_Experience}, with an interaction model performing better. \\
Notably, \texttt{Years\_Since\_Graduation} was associated with employment status but not salary. The dataset itself cannot explain this pattern, since it records no information on what graduates actually do during this time. One plausible interpretation is that time since graduation proxies for accumulated job-search experience, self-directed preparation, or growing familiarity with the labor market, though this remains a hypothesis rather than a finding this analysis can directly test. For salary, a generalized additive model (GAM) outperformed a linear model, revealing a non-linear relationship between GPA and salary. Overall, these findings suggest that employment status and salary for international graduates depend on combination of academic performance, practical experience and institutional reputation, rather than any single factor.

\subsection{Limitations}

Several should be acknowledged. \texttt{Employment\_Status} was imbalanced between classes, although the classification tree was the best-performing model overall it still misses a meaningful proportion of genuinely employed graduates as a result. In addition, the dataset was dominated by categorical variables, with relatively few continuous predictors available, which may have limited how well relationships such as salary variation could be captured and modeled.

\subsection{Recommendations}

To frame these findings, it is worth noting that this dissertation's  findings align closely with those of an employability research internship at the College of Science and Engineering, University of Glasgow, in which I personally participated. The internship aimed to understand the gap between students' and employers' expectations about employability and to identify what the university need to do to help students transition more successfully into employment. The qualitative analysis of interview data from students and employers in that internship and the quantitative analysis of this dissertation reinforce one another.  In particular, both pieces of work point toward the importance of actual work experience: embedding internships within the curriculum rather than treating them as optional and providing careers support that is tailored to each school or discipline. Together, this gives universities a strong evidence base for prioritizing these two areas to work to close the gap between the student experience and labor market demands. 
